[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 968,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 733,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139815,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 733,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ewing sarcoma of the orbital bone is a small round cell sarcoma containing a fusion between one FET family gene (usually <em>EWSR1</em>) and one ETS family gene (most often <em>FLI1</em>).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 969,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 734,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139816,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9364/3 Ewing sarcoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139817,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2B52.Y Ewing sarcoma of bone or articular cartilage of other specified sites</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139818,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> (peripheral) primitive neuroectodermal tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139819,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139820,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ewing sarcoma of the orbital area is more likely to arise from orbital bone (83%) than soft tissue (17%) {29219959}. Extraorbital extension occurs in most patients and is mainly intracranial.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139821,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common presenting complaints were proptosis and upper eyelid swelling in the largest reported series {29219959}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139822,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ewing sarcoma of the orbit occurs most commonly in children and young adults {29219959}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139823,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The majority of cases are sporadic. Although germline mutations (in <em>BRCA1</em>, for example) have been detected {29977059}, their pathogenetic roles remain unclear.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139824,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most tumours harbour a t(11;22)(q24;q12) translocation {6713356;6713357}, resulting in <em>EWSR1</em>::<em>FLI1</em> fusion {29977059}. The second most common translocation is t(21;22)(q22;q12), which results in <em>EWSR1</em>::<em>ERG</em> fusion {29977059}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139825,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The cut surface of untreated Ewing sarcoma specimens is greyish-white and soft, and it frequently includes areas of haemorrhage and necrosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139826,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours consist of a monotonous population of small round cells arranged in sheets or solid aggregates, separated by fibrous septa. Focal rosettes are sometimes present. Cells have round nuclei, coarse chromatin, small nucleoli, and scant eosinophilic or clear cytoplasm. Mitotic activity is brisk and necrosis is common.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>There is diffuse, strong, membranous CD99 expression and nuclear FLI1 expression. NKX2-2 has a higher specificity than CD99 {29661713}. Focal positivity with broad-spectrum cytokeratins is common. Desmin, myogenin, and MYOD1 are negative.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139827,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 744,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 980,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 745,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139828,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 745,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>All cases of Ewing sarcoma harbour a FET::ETS fusion. Genetic confirmation is often required for Ewing sarcoma diagnosis. The most common Ewing sarcoma fusion (present in ~85% of cases) is <em>EWSR1</em>::<em>FLI1</em>. The second most common is <em>EWSR1</em>::<em>ERG</em> in about 10% of cases. The remaining cases have alternative translocations that join either <em>EWSR1</em> or <em>FUS</em> (which, along with <em>TAF15</em>, form the FET family) to other ETS family members.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 983,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 748,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139829,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 748,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> small round cell morphology; strong CD99 membranous expression.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> strong and diffuse NKX2-2 expression; detection of a FET::ETS fusion.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 981,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 746,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139830,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 746,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) and Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM systems can be applied.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 982,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 747,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139831,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 304,
        "headingId": 747,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The prognosis for Ewing sarcoma has improved considerably with current multimodal therapy, with a 65&ndash;70% cure rate for localized disease. However, metastatic and early relapsing tumours have a poor prognosis, with a 5-year survival rate of &lt;&nbsp;30%. The presence of metastases appears to be the main prognostic factor. Other prognostic factors include the anatomical location of the tumour, tumour volume, and the patient&rsquo;s age {29977059}. Complete pathological response to neoadjuvant chemotherapy is a favourable prognostic factor {27482030}. There are currently no other prognostic markers in widespread use.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:08:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]